  "reason": "1) Evidence/uncertainty: No experiments have been completed, so there is zero information about whether the data contains usable signal, how strong a simple baseline is, or which design axes (modality, profile, encoder, aggregation, fusion) will matter. 2) What this tests: config_001 (rna_only, singleprofile, pca_n2000) is the simplest possible configuration—single modality, single profile, a lightweight linear encoder, no aggregation or fusion. It establishes the performance floor and confirms whether the task is learnable at all with basic RNA features. 3) Why it is the best first use of budget: Starting from the minimal baseline gives every subsequent experiment a clean reference point. If config_001 performs reasonably, later experiments can isolate the marginal contribution of adding multiprofile, richer encoders (scvi, scgpt), image modality, or fusion. If it performs poorly, we learn early that representation quality is the critical bottleneck and can prioritize encoder choice before investing in fusion or aggregation. This maximizes information per experiment by anchoring the entire search to a well-understood reference, and it avoids the risk of interpreting a complex multimodal result without knowing what the simple components achieve."
